    {
      "id": "e53171bea174275ad2500078004316b23563f3b5",
      "individual_note": true,
      "resolvable": false,
      "notes": [
        {
          "id": 2939783740,
          "type": null,
          "body": "Maybe the best way to look at zero is as a deregistered field after all. I was confused by that but if you wanted to know how many field IDs there are total, to allocate an array for example, you'd want to include it. To know the number of valid fields it shouldn't be included. Both numbers are useful but to get the second value you could subtract deregistered_count from total_count and that always works.\n\n@johnthacker Should I restore the previous counting semantics and treat zero as \"deregistered\" after all?\n\nI'm concerned total_count - zero will lead to subtle bugs and off-by-one errors.",
          "author": {
            "id": 2639362,
            "username": "jvalverde",
            "public_email": "",
            "name": "João Valverde",
            "state": "active",
            "locked": false,
            "avatar_url": "https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/bea5f745397cf5a2fe0526df96ec143db47f9334340ab56515038832be57e976?s=80&d=identicon",
            "web_url": "https://gitlab.com/jvalverde"
          },
          "created_at": "2025-12-08T15:30:05.074Z",
          "updated_at": "2025-12-08T15:30:05.074Z",
          "system": false,
          "noteable_id": 438706450,
          "noteable_type": "MergeRequest",
          "project_id": 7898047,
          "resolvable": false,
          "confidential": false,
          "internal": false,
          "imported": false,
          "imported_from": "none",
          "noteable_iid": 22675,
          "commands_changes": {}
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "be69e5dab3c0df098dd8b1618a82caf3128f4322",
      "individual_note": true,
      "resolvable": false,
      "notes": [
        {
          "id": 2939802708,
          "type": null,
          "body": "I am a bit concerned that there can be off by one errors either way. It's inherent when we suddenly have one 1-indexed array and the number of fields and index of the last field have a different relationship than expected. Not sure the best way to handle it.",
          "author": {
            "id": 6764551,
            "username": "johnthacker",
            "public_email": "",
            "name": "John Thacker",
            "state": "active",
            "locked": false,
            "avatar_url": "https://secure.gravatar.com/avatar/99c30adacedf45514aaf711ba8a44f56322ebb53e9b550b7e3c9cd83bd6a1408?s=80&d=identicon",
            "web_url": "https://gitlab.com/johnthacker"
          },
          "created_at": "2025-12-08T15:36:23.957Z",
          "updated_at": "2025-12-08T15:36:23.957Z",
          "system": false,
          "noteable_id": 438706450,
          "noteable_type": "MergeRequest",
          "project_id": 7898047,
          "resolvable": false,
          "confidential": false,
          "internal": false,
          "imported": false,
          "imported_from": "none",
          "noteable_iid": 22675,
          "commands_changes": {}
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "f3f0c4dcff4d785c4b68f9b72b6bae8250424b78",
      "individual_note": true,
      "resolvable": false,
      "notes": [
        {
          "id": 2939833537,
          "type": null,
          "body": "Right. A better defense is probably to be consistent about using 1-indexing, and not having 0-indexing with a somewhat weird deregistered field.",
          "author": {
            "id": 2639362,
            "username": "jvalverde",
            "public_email": "",
            "name": "João Valverde",
